1
00:00:00,460 --> 00:00:11,540
The World Health Organization has categorized biological agents into four risk groups.

2
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:20,540
Biological agents categorized under Risk Group 1 pose a low individual risk and a low community risk.

3
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:27,540
This category comprises microorganisms that are unlikely to cause human or animal disease.

4
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:39,020
Biological agents categorized as Risk Group 2 pose a moderate individual risk, a low community risk.

5
00:00:39,020 --> 00:00:47,020
Pathogens that can cause human or animal disease but are unlikely to be a serious hazard to laboratory workers,

6
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:52,020
the community, livestock, or the environment.

7
00:00:52,020 --> 00:00:59,020
Laboratory exposures to Risk Group 2 biological agents may cause severe infection.

8
00:00:59,020 --> 00:01:04,020
However, effective treatment and preventive measures are available.

9
00:01:04,020 --> 00:01:08,020
The risk of spread of infection is limited.

10
00:01:08,020 --> 00:01:19,380
Biological agents categorized under Risk Group 3 pose a high individual risk, a low community risk.

11
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:30,380
A pathogen that usually causes serious human or animal disease but does not ordinarily spread from one infected individual to another.

12
00:01:30,380 --> 00:01:34,380
Effective treatment and preventive measures are available.

13
00:01:34,380 --> 00:01:46,620
A biological agent categorized under Risk Group 4 poses a high individual risk and a high community risk.

14
00:01:46,620 --> 00:01:58,620
A pathogen that usually causes serious human or animal disease and it can be readily transmitted from one individual to another directly or indirectly.

15
00:01:58,620 --> 00:02:05,340
Effective treatment and preventive measures are not usually available.

16
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:14,340
The following regional factors must be taken into account when developing a risk assessment and risk mitigation strategy.

17
00:02:14,340 --> 00:02:24,340
The regional factors include the pathogenicity of the organism and the variants which have evolved locally.

18
00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:35,340
The mode of transmission and the host range of the organism, local immunity, the density of the population, the mobility of the population,

19
00:02:36,340 --> 00:02:43,340
the presence of vectors and other environmental factors such as the local climatic conditions.

20
00:02:43,340 --> 00:02:55,620
And other two factors which must be considered are the local availability of effective preventive measures and the local availability of effective treatment.
